member.example:
Example on how to use TrustedStoreConstraint.

    Code walk-through:

        PeAuthenticodeVerifier.Verify(IFileLock) =>
            1. This method calls WinVerifyTrust to validate Authenticode signing of the file.
            2. This method (internally) will get back a list of the signing certificates that signed the file.
            3. If Authenticode signing validation passed, then all signing certificate information is passed to the constraint.

        PeAuthenticodeVerifier.Constraints.Verify(IValidationInfo) -- which is actually TrustedStoreConstraint.Verify(IValidationInfo) =>
            1. This method loops thru the validation info (signing certificates) and called CertificateStore.Contains(X509Certificate2) for each certificate.
            2. If CertificateStore.Contains(X509Certificate2) returns true then a trusted certificate was found and this constraint returns true.

        This means if PeAuthenticodeVerifier.Verify(IFileLock) returns Win32ErrorCodes.ERROR_SUCCESS
        then authenticode validation passed and at least one signing cert was found in the trusted store.

        If PeAuthenticodeVerifier.Verify(IFileLock) returns something other than Win32ErrorCodes.ERROR_SUCCESS
        then either authenticode validation failed or none of the signing certificates were found in the trusted store.
        Note, process the return code from this method to determine the reason for failure.


    var store = new CertificateStore("DansTrustedCerts");

    var verifier = new PeAuthenticodeVerifier
    {
        Constraints = new TrustedStoreConstraint(store);
    }

    // Hold scope between validation and handling of the file before releasing the file lock to protect against time-of-check/time-of-use (TOCTOU)
    using (var fileLock = new FileLock(@"C:\temp\somefile.exe"))
    {
        var returnCode = verifier.Verify(fileLock);
        switch (returnCode)
        {
            case Win32ErrorCodes.ERROR_SUCCESS:
                // Perform handling of the file here...
                // When the file is no longer needed, exit this scope to release the file lock
                Console.WriteLine("File passed authenticode check and signed by something in our trusted certificate store");
                break;

            case Win32ErrorCodes.TRUST_E_EXPLICIT_DISTRUST:
                Console.Error.WriteLine("File was not signed by a certificate in our trusted store");
                break;

            default:
                Console.Error.WriteLine($"Authenticode validation failed - error code: {returnCode}");
        }
    }

